<Base title="Defensive Gaps: Recognizing Cutting Opportunities | Bence Supola"><div class="container"><div class="case-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Case study 01 · Spatial feature engineering / Explainable ML</div><h1>Recognizing When a Defensive Gap Opens</h1><p class="lead">Can player-tracking data reveal an opportunity to cut to the basket during a teammate’s drive — including opportunities a player does not take?</p><div class="skills"><span class="skill">SportVU · 25 Hz</span><span class="skill">Geometric features</span><span class="skill">Gradient boosting</span><span class="skill">Partial dependence / SHAP</span></div></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>0.818</strong><span>Balanced accuracy in study evaluation</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>138.7 ms</strong><span>Average lead relative to observed cut starts</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>5</strong><span>Engineered spatial features</span></div></div><div class="prose"><h2>The basketball problem</h2><p>Cutting behind a shifting defense can produce an efficient shot, yet a missed cutting opportunity leaves no obvious event in a box score. The research problem was therefore not to predict which choice a player would make, but to <strong>recognize when the defensive geometry permits a cut</strong> during a teammate’s drive. That makes the target difficult to label: observed cuts are examples of exploited openings, whereas a player remaining outside may reflect either defensive denial or a tactical choice.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>Turning tactical intuition into measurable geometry</h2><p>We expressed a defender’s position relative to the potential cutter’s straight path to the basket using <code>h</code> (lateral distance from that path) and <code>c</code> (the projected defender position along the path relative to the basket). The features <code>h_change</code>, <code>c_change</code> and <code>c_relative</code> added movement and relative context. This representation was designed to encode <em>how</em> a lane opens, rather than asking a model to discover basketball geometry from raw coordinates alone.</p><p>Building a meaningful training set required manually selecting 100 positive cutting actions and 100 negative drive-and-kick actions where the defender appeared to deny a cut. Positive and negative examples were sampled at relevant moments within the drive; labeling every frame indiscriminately would obscure the short-lived opening.</p></div><figure class="figure figure-wide"><a href="/images/defensive-sequence.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/defensive-sequence.png" alt="Three panels of a reconstructed drive, pass and shot with player locations and annotated actions." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><strong>Tracking-based action reconstruction.</strong> An unpublished visualization prepared for LinkedIn from the defensive-gaps research. It illustrates the event context and data-processing workflow; the panels do not themselves demonstrate a predicted cutting opportunity.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>Why gradient boosting — and how it was evaluated</h2><p>Five interdependent geometric variables, non-linear relationships and a relatively small, carefully labeled action set favored an interpretable tree-based approach. The study used gradient-boosted decision trees rather than treating the task as a large-scale image or sequence prediction problem.</p><p>To reduce leakage between closely related frames, evaluation separated <em>actions</em> in five-fold cross-validation rather than randomly splitting individual tracking frames. The frame-level classifier used the primary defender for training; action-level assessment considered obstacles from other defenders as well.</p><p>The reported balanced accuracy was <strong>0.818</strong>. For detected cutting actions, the study reported an average detection lead of <strong>138.7 ms</strong> relative to annotated cut starts. These are results for the paper’s curated evaluation, not a guarantee of generalization to every possession or a measure of whether a player should have cut.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>Explaining the learned geometry</h2><p>A useful detector should be interpretable to coaches and analysts. The published two-feature partial dependence plots examine how pairs of engineered features relate to the fitted model’s response while averaging over other inputs. They help investigate non-linear relationships; they do not establish causal effects or prove that a particular movement would force an opening.</p></div><figure class="figure figure-wide"><a href="/images/defensive-partial-dependence.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/defensive-partial-dependence.png" alt="Four published three-dimensional two-feature partial dependence surfaces for geometric features c, h and their changes." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><strong>Two-feature partial dependence, published Figure 4.</strong> The four surfaces illustrate learned relationships between spatial features. Panel color scales differ. Source: Supola, Hoch &amp; Baca (2023), <em>PLOS ONE</em>, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0281467">DOI</a>; <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Reproduced without analytical modification.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>What the result contributes</h2><p>The study proposes a way to operationalize an otherwise subjective tactical concept: a potential cutting lane that may exist even without a cut. Its value is in generating analyzable signals and interpretable feedback for reviewing off-ball movement — not in forecasting individual intentions or establishing optimal decisions.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>My role and scope</h2><p>I implemented the programming and analytical workflow underpinning this collaborative research. The study design, scientific interpretation and publication were developed with my coauthors.</p><p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0281467">Read the peer-reviewed paper ↗</a> · <a href="/#work">Back to selected work</a></p></div></div></Base>
